OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026", "title": "The logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski conjecture", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "091", "family_title": "Logarithmic and <i>L</i><sub><i>p</i></sub> Brunn–Minkowski inequalities and the B-conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove the logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski conjecture for arbitrary origin-symmetric convex bodies in every dimension. The theorem also gives the symmetric <i>L</i><sub><i>p</i></sub> Brunn–Minkowski inequality for every \\(0\\lt p\\lt 1\\). Combined with Saroglou's transfer theorem and a support-subspace reduction, it yields the logarithmic inequality for every even log-concave Radon measure and the scalar-dilation \\((B)\\)-conjecture.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Even logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski inequality — original Theorem 1.1.", "statement": "<p>Even logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski inequality — original Theorem 1.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq 1\\). A convex body in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) is a compact convex set with nonempty interior.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K,L\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be convex bodies satisfying \\(K=-K\\) and \\(L=-L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a convex body \\(C\\), define its support function by\n  \\[\n  h_C(u)=\\max_{x\\in C}\\langle x,u\\rangle,\\qquad u\\in S^{n-1}.\n  \\]\n  The support functions of \\(K\\) and \\(L\\) are strictly positive.</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive continuous function \\(g:S^{n-1}\\to(0,\\infty)\\), define its Wulff body by\n  \\[\n  W[g]=\\bigcap_{u\\in S^{n-1}}\n  \\{x\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\langle x,u\\rangle\\leq g(u)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(|C|\\) for the \\(n\\)-dimensional Lebesgue measure of \\(C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0\\leq\\lambda\\leq1\\),\n\\[\n\\left|W[h_K^{1-\\lambda}h_L^\\lambda]\\right|\n\\geq |K|^{1-\\lambda}|L|^\\lambda.\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\nThe intersection defining the Wulff body is used whether or not \\(h_K^{1-\\lambda}h_L^\\lambda\\) is itself a support function.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose first that \\(n\\geq2\\). Fix nonzero spanning vectors \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_N\\) and positive endpoint widths \\(h_i^0,h_i^1\\). For \\(t\\in[0,1]\\), define\n\\[\nh_i(t)=(h_i^0)^{1-t}(h_i^1)^t,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nP(t)=\n\\{x:|\\langle p_i,x\\rangle|\\leq h_i(t)\\text{ for all }i\\}.\n\\]\nThe spanning condition makes every \\(P(t)\\) bounded; positivity of the widths gives nonempty interior.</p><p>For an even integer \\(q\\geq2\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), define\n\\[\n\\psi_i(t,x)\n=\n\\left(\\frac{\\langle p_i,x\\rangle}{h_i(t)}\\right)^q,\n\\qquad\nV(t,x)=\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}|x|^2+\\sum_i\\psi_i(t,x),\n\\]\n\\[\nZ_{q,\\varepsilon}(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-V(t,x)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nd\\mu_t=Z_{q,\\varepsilon}(t)^{-1}e^{-V(t,x)}\\,dx.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(q,\\varepsilon\\), the first two derivatives may be taken under the integral: on a compact interval of \\(t\\), the differentiated integrands are bounded by a polynomial times \\(e^{-\\varepsilon|x|^2/2}\\). With\n\\[\nb_i=-q\\log(h_i^1/h_i^0),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\dot V=\\sum_i b_i\\psi_i,\n\\qquad\n\\ddot V=\\sum_i b_i^2\\psi_i.\n\\]\nConsequently, Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d^2}{dt^2}\\log Z_{q,\\varepsilon}(t)\n=\n\\operatorname{Var}_{\\mu_t}(\\dot V)\n-\n\\mathbb E_{\\mu_t}\\ddot V\n\\leq0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nZ_{q,\\varepsilon}(\\lambda)\n\\geq\nZ_{q,\\varepsilon}(0)^{1-\\lambda}\nZ_{q,\\varepsilon}(1)^\\lambda.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]</p><p>Keep \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) fixed and let \\(q\\to\\infty\\) through even integers. For each fixed \\(t\\), the integrands converge almost everywhere to\n\\[\ne^{-\\varepsilon|x|^2/2}1_{P(t)}.\n\\]\nThe exceptional set lies in the finitely many hyperplanes\n\\[\n\\langle p_i,x\\rangle=\\pm h_i(t)\n\\]\nand has measure zero. The common integrable bound \\(e^{-\\varepsilon|x|^2/2}\\) gives\n\\[\nZ_{q,\\varepsilon}(t)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\int_{P(t)}e^{-\\varepsilon|x|^2/2}\\,dx.\n\\]\nApply this at \\(t=0,\\lambda,1\\) in (2.3), then let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). Monotone convergence gives\n\\[\n|P(\\lambda)|\n\\geq\n|P(0)|^{1-\\lambda}|P(1)|^\\lambda.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]</p><p>To pass to all directions, choose nested finite sets\n\\[\nD_m\\subset S^{n-1}\n\\]\nthat contain the coordinate vectors and whose union is dense in the sphere. Define\n\\[\nP_m(t)=\n\\bigcap_{p\\in D_m}\n\\{x:|\\langle p,x\\rangle|\n\\leq h_K(p)^{1-t}h_L(p)^t\\}.\n\\]\nFor each \\(t\\), the bodies \\(P_m(t)\\) decrease with \\(m\\) and are all contained in the bounded body \\(P_1(t)\\). By continuity and evenness of the support functions,\n\\[\n\\bigcap_mP_m(t)=W[h_K^{1-t}h_L^t].\n\\]\nIndeed, any point satisfying the dense set of inequalities satisfies all of them by continuity in the direction. Conversely, the Wulff inequalities imply the absolute-value constraints by evenness.</p><p>At \\(t=0,1\\), these intersections are \\(K,L\\), respectively, by the supporting-half-space characterization of a convex body. Apply (2.4) to each \\(P_m\\), and then use continuity of measure from above at \\(t=0,\\lambda,1\\). This proves\n\\[\n|W[h_K^{1-\\lambda}h_L^\\lambda]|\n\\geq |K|^{1-\\lambda}|L|^\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>In dimension one, write\n\\[\nK=[-a,a],\\qquad L=[-b,b],\n\\qquad a,b&gt;0.\n\\]\nTheir Wulff interpolation is the interval with radius \\(a^{1-\\lambda}b^\\lambda\\), whose length is\n\\[\n(2a)^{1-\\lambda}(2b)^\\lambda.\n\\]\nThus (1.1) is equality in dimension one. The endpoints \\(\\lambda=0,1\\) are equality in every dimension.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Symmetric \\(L_p\\) Brunn–Minkowski inequality — original Corollary 1.2.", "statement": "<p>Symmetric \\(L_p\\) Brunn–Minkowski inequality — original Corollary 1.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq1\\), and let \\(K,L\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be full-dimensional origin-symmetric convex bodies.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;p&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the support functions, Wulff bodies, and Lebesgue-volume notation defined in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0\\leq\\lambda\\leq1\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\nW\\!\\left[\\big((1-\\lambda)h_K^p+\\lambda h_L^p\\big)^{1/p}\\right]\n\\right|^{p/n}\n\\geq\n(1-\\lambda)|K|^{p/n}+\\lambda|L|^{p/n}.\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(\\lambda=0,1\\), the assertion follows from\n\\[\nW[h_K]=K,\\qquad W[h_L]=L.\n\\]\nSuppose \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\). Set\n\\[\na=|K|^{1/n}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nb=|L|^{1/n}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nK_0=a^{-1}K,\n\\qquad\nL_0=b^{-1}L,\n\\]\nso that \\(|K_0|=|L_0|=1\\). Define\n\\[\nc=\\big((1-\\lambda)a^p+\\lambda b^p\\big)^{1/p}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n\\theta=\\frac{\\lambda b^p}{c^p}\\in(0,1).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n1-\\theta=\\frac{(1-\\lambda)a^p}{c^p}.\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(u\\in S^{n-1}\\), the support identities\n\\[\nh_K=ah_{K_0},\n\\qquad\nh_L=bh_{L_0},\n\\]\nfollowed by weighted arithmetic–geometric mean, give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\big((1-\\lambda)h_K(u)^p+\\lambda h_L(u)^p\\big)^{1/p}\n&amp;=\nc\\big((1-\\theta)h_{K_0}(u)^p\n+\\theta h_{L_0}(u)^p\\big)^{1/p}\\\\\n&amp;\\geq\nc\\,h_{K_0}(u)^{1-\\theta}h_{L_0}(u)^\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll support values are positive. The Wulff definition is monotone in its support bound and satisfies\n\\[\nW[cg]=cW[g],\\qquad c&gt;0.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\ncW[h_{K_0}^{1-\\theta}h_{L_0}^{\\theta}]\n\\subset\nW\\!\\left[\\big((1-\\lambda)h_K^p+\\lambda h_L^p\\big)^{1/p}\\right].\n\\]</p><p>By Theorem 1, the unscaled body on the left has volume at least \\(1\\), because \\(K_0,L_0\\) are origin-symmetric and have unit volume. The body on the right therefore has volume at least \\(c^n\\). Raising this bound to the positive power \\(p/n\\) gives (1.2), since\n\\[\nc^p=(1-\\lambda)|K|^{p/n}+\\lambda|L|^{p/n}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Measure inequality and scalar \\((B)\\)-conjecture — original Corollary 8.1.", "statement": "<p>Measure inequality and scalar \\((B)\\)-conjecture — original Corollary 8.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq1\\), and let \\(\\mu\\) be a nonnegative even log-concave Radon measure on \\(\\mathbb R^n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Here log-concavity means that, for every pair of compact sets \\(A,B\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) and every \\(0&lt;\\theta&lt;1\\),\n  \\[\n  \\mu((1-\\theta)A+\\theta B)\n  \\geq \\mu(A)^{1-\\theta}\\mu(B)^\\theta.\n  \\]\n  Evenness means \\(\\mu(A)=\\mu(-A)\\) for every Borel set \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K,L\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be origin-symmetric convex bodies.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the support functions and Wulff bodies defined in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Endpoint products in interpolation inequalities are understood as the corresponding endpoint values. Log-concavity of nonnegative functions is understood multiplicatively, including the identically zero function.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0\\leq\\lambda\\leq1\\),\n\\[\n\\mu\\!\\left(W[h_K^{1-\\lambda}h_L^\\lambda]\\right)\n\\geq \\mu(K)^{1-\\lambda}\\mu(L)^\\lambda.\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nConsequently, for every origin-symmetric convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), the function\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\ni t\\longmapsto\\mu(e^tK)\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nis log-concave.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\), and write\n\\[\nD=W[h_K^{1-\\lambda}h_L^\\lambda].\n\\]\nThe assertion is immediate when \\(\\mu\\) is the zero measure.</p><p>Otherwise, choose a closed ball \\(C\\) centered at the origin, large enough that\n\\[\nK\\cup L\\cup D\\subset C,\n\\qquad\n0&lt;\\mu(C)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThis is possible because the three bodies are compact and \\(\\mu\\) is a nonzero Radon measure. The probability measure\n\\[\n\\nu(A)=\\frac{\\mu(A\\cap C)}{\\mu(C)}\n\\]\nis even and log-concave. Indeed, for compact \\(A,B\\), convexity of \\(C\\) gives\n\\[\n(1-\\theta)(A\\cap C)+\\theta(B\\cap C)\n\\subset\n((1-\\theta)A+\\theta B)\\cap C,\n\\]\nand the defining inequality for \\(\\mu\\) applies. Since \\(K,L,D\\subset C\\), the common factor \\(\\mu(C)\\) cancels from (8.1). It therefore suffices to prove the inequality for \\(\\nu\\).</p><p>Let \\(E\\) be the affine hull of \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\nu\\). Evenness gives \\(E=-E\\), so \\(E\\) is a linear subspace. If \\(\\dim E=0\\), then \\(\\nu=\\delta_0\\); all three bodies contain \\(0\\), and equality holds.</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\nd=\\dim E\\geq1,\n\\]\nand identify \\(E\\) isometrically with \\(\\mathbb R^d\\). By inner regularity, the compact-set definition of log-concavity for \\(\\nu\\) is equivalent to the Borel-set formulation using inner measure. Borell’s characterization [4, Theorem 1.1, with \\(s=0\\)], applied to \\(\\nu\\) on \\(E\\), represents it as an affine image of a log-concave density in a dimension \\(k\\leq d\\). Since its support spans \\(E\\), the affine map has rank \\(d\\). Thus \\(k=d\\), the map is invertible, and \\(\\nu\\) has a log-concave density \\(f\\) with respect to Lebesgue measure on \\(E\\).</p><p>Evenness of \\(\\nu\\) gives\n\\[\nf(x)=f(-x)\n\\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n\\]\nReplacing \\(f(x)\\) by \\(\\sqrt{f(x)f(-x)}\\) gives an even log-concave representative of the same density.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nK_E=K\\cap E,\n\\qquad\nL_E=L\\cap E.\n\\]\nThese are origin-symmetric convex bodies in \\(E\\), since \\(K,L\\) contain neighborhoods of \\(0\\). Define their relative support functions for \\(v\\in E\\) by\n\\[\nh^E_{K_E}(v)=\\max_{y\\in K_E}\\langle y,v\\rangle,\n\\qquad\nh^E_{L_E}(v)=\\max_{y\\in L_E}\\langle y,v\\rangle.\n\\]\nTheir relative Wulff body is\n\\[\nD_E\n=\n\\bigcap_{v\\in E}\n\\left\\{\nx\\in E:\n\\langle x,v\\rangle\n\\leq\nh^E_{K_E}(v)^{1-\\lambda}h^E_{L_E}(v)^\\lambda\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nUsing all \\(v\\in E\\), rather than only unit vectors, gives the same body by homogeneity.</p><p>Let \\(P_E\\) be the orthogonal projection onto \\(E\\). For \\(x\\in D_E\\) and \\(u\\in S^{n-1}\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\langle x,u\\rangle\n&amp;=\\langle x,P_Eu\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\nh^E_{K_E}(P_Eu)^{1-\\lambda}\nh^E_{L_E}(P_Eu)^\\lambda\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\nh_K(u)^{1-\\lambda}h_L(u)^\\lambda.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\nh^E_{K_E}(P_Eu)\n=\n\\max_{y\\in K\\cap E}\\langle y,u\\rangle\n\\leq h_K(u),\n\\]\nand likewise for \\(L\\); if \\(P_Eu=0\\), the middle product is \\(0\\). Thus\n\\[\nD_E\\subset D\\cap E.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]</p><p>Theorem 1 in dimension \\(d\\), followed by Saroglou’s transfer theorem [29, Theorem 3.1] in that same dimension, gives\n\\[\n\\nu(D_E)\n\\geq\n\\nu(K_E)^{1-\\lambda}\\nu(L_E)^\\lambda.\n\\]\nThe transfer theorem states that the logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski inequality for Lebesgue measure in dimension \\(d\\) implies the same Wulff inequality for every even log-concave density in dimension \\(d\\); the density may vanish and need not be normalized.</p><p>Since \\(\\nu\\) is supported on \\(E\\), inclusion (8.3) yields\n\\[\n\\nu(D)\n=\n\\nu(D\\cap E)\n\\geq\n\\nu(D_E)\n\\geq\n\\nu(K)^{1-\\lambda}\\nu(L)^\\lambda.\n\\]\nThis proves (8.1) for \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\). At \\(\\lambda=0,1\\), the Wulff bodies are \\(K,L\\), respectively, and the endpoint convention gives equality.</p><p>Finally, for \\(s,t\\in\\mathbb R\\), the support identity\n\\[\nh_{e^sK}=e^sh_K\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nW[h_{e^sK}^{1-\\lambda}h_{e^tK}^{\\lambda}]\n=\nW[e^{(1-\\lambda)s+\\lambda t}h_K]\n=\ne^{(1-\\lambda)s+\\lambda t}K.\n\\]\nApplying (8.1) to \\(e^sK\\) and \\(e^tK\\) proves\n\\[\n\\mu(e^{(1-\\lambda)s+\\lambda t}K)\n\\geq\n\\mu(e^sK)^{1-\\lambda}\\mu(e^tK)^\\lambda.\n\\]\nThis is exactly the log-concavity in (8.2), and is the scalar-dilation argument of Saroglou [29, Corollary 3.2].</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Moment coordinates — original Lemma 3.1.", "statement": "<p>Moment coordinates — original Lemma 3.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq2\\), \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and let \\(V\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n)\\) be even with\n  \\[\n  D^2V\\geq\\varepsilon I.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  Z=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx,\n  \\qquad\n  d\\mu(x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an even \\(\\varphi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n)\\) such that \\(d\\nu(z)=e^{-\\varphi(z)}\\,dz\\) is a probability measure, \\(\\nabla\\varphi\\) is a diffeomorphism of \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) onto \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), and\n\\[\n(\\nabla\\varphi)_\\#\\nu=\\mu,\n\\qquad\n0&lt;D^2\\varphi\\leq\\frac4\\varepsilon I,\n\\qquad\n\\log\\det D^2\\varphi\n=\nV(\\nabla\\varphi)+\\log Z-\\varphi.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\varphi(z)\\geq c|z|-b\n\\]\nfor some \\(c&gt;0\\) and \\(b&lt;\\infty\\). The lower Hessian inequality means positive definiteness at every point; it need not be uniform on \\(\\mathbb R^n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemmas 1.1 and 1.2. It remains to obtain a smooth limit and verify that its gradient reaches every point.</p><p>On a fixed ball, choose the uniform Hessian interval \\([\\delta,C]\\) from Lemma 1.2. Choose a smooth concave scalar function \\(s\\) equal to \\(\\log\\) on a slightly larger interval than \\([\\delta,C]\\), with \\(s'\\) bounded above and bounded below by positive constants on \\(\\mathbb R\\). Such an extension is obtained by smoothly continuing the decreasing derivative \\(1/t\\) to positive constant tails.</p><p>For symmetric matrices \\(Q\\), the spectral function\n\\[\nF(Q)=\\operatorname{tr}s(Q)\n\\]\nis smooth, concave, and uniformly elliptic: its derivative has eigenvalues \\(s'(\\lambda_i(Q))\\), and its second variation has coefficients \\(s''(\\lambda_i)\\) and the nonpositive divided differences of \\(s'\\). It agrees with \\(\\log\\det\\) on the Hessians under consideration. The nonhomogeneous Evans–Krylov estimate [33, Corollary 2.3], applied to\n\\[\nF(D^2\\varphi_R)=f_R,\n\\]\ngives uniform interior \\(C^{2,\\alpha}\\) bounds for some \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\).</p><p>Each \\(\\varphi_R\\) is already smooth. Its derivative \\(w_{R,k}=\\partial_k\\varphi_R\\) satisfies\n\\[\n(D^2\\varphi_R)^{-1}:D^2w_{R,k}\n=\n\\nabla V(\\nabla\\varphi_R)\\cdot\\nabla w_{R,k}-w_{R,k},\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q:N=\\operatorname{tr}(Q^TN)\\). The principal coefficients and the right-hand side are uniformly \\(C^\\alpha\\) on smaller balls. Interior Schauder estimates [16, Theorem 2.20 and Corollary 2.21] give uniform \\(C^{3,\\alpha}\\) bounds for \\(\\varphi_R\\), and iteration gives bounds for every derivative on compact sets.</p><p>A diagonal subsequence, still indexed by \\(R\\), converges locally smoothly to an even \\(\\varphi\\) with positive definite Hessian, the same upper cap, and\n\\[\n\\log\\det D^2\\varphi\n=\nV(\\nabla\\varphi)+\\log Z-\\varphi.\n\\]\nThe bound\n\\[\n\\varphi_R(z)\\geq c|z|-b,\n\\]\nwith \\(b\\) independent of \\(R\\), also passes to \\(\\varphi\\).</p><p>The common exponential majorant proves\n\\[\n\\int e^{-\\varphi}=1\n\\]\nby dominated convergence. For each bounded continuous \\(h\\), it also gives\n\\[\n\\int h(\\nabla\\varphi)e^{-\\varphi}\n=\n\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int h(\\nabla\\varphi_R)e^{-\\varphi_R}\n=\n\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int h\\,d\\mu_R\n=\n\\int h\\,d\\mu.\n\\]\nThus \\((\\nabla\\varphi)_\\#\\nu=\\mu\\).</p><p>Positive definite Hessian makes the gradient locally invertible and injective, since for \\(z\\neq w\\),\n\\[\n(\\nabla\\varphi(z)-\\nabla\\varphi(w))\\cdot(z-w)\n=\n\\int_0^1\nD^2\\varphi(w+t(z-w))[z-w,z-w]\\,dt\n&gt;0.\n\\]\nIts image is dense: an open ball missed by the image would have zero pushforward mass, contrary to the everywhere positive density of \\(\\mu\\).</p><p>Fix \\(x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n\\). Density permits finitely many image points\n\\[\ny_j=\\nabla\\varphi(z_j)\n\\]\nwhose convex hull contains a ball \\(B_r(x_0)\\) with \\(r&gt;0\\); one may take small perturbations of the points \\(x_0\\pm e_i\\). Their supporting planes imply\n\\[\n\\varphi(z)-x_0\\cdot z\n\\geq\n\\max_j\\{(y_j-x_0)\\cdot z+\\varphi(z_j)-y_j\\cdot z_j\\}\n\\geq r|z|-b_{x_0}.\n\\]\nThis function attains a minimum, at which \\(\\nabla\\varphi=x_0\\). The gradient is therefore onto, and its local smooth inverses combine to a global smooth inverse.</p><p>In target coordinates,\n\\[\n\\tau(x)=D_z^2\\varphi((\\nabla\\varphi)^{-1}(x))\n\\]\nis smooth, even, positive definite, and bounded above by \\((4/\\varepsilon)I\\). The gradient is odd, as is its inverse. A compactly supported smooth function of \\(x\\) remains compactly supported after composition with \\(\\nabla\\varphi\\), because its support lies in the image of a compact set under the continuous inverse map.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Compactly supported moment measures.", "statement": "<p>Compactly supported moment measures.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit \\(n,\\varepsilon,V,Z,\\mu\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(R\\geq2\\), write\n  \\[\n  B_R=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^n:|x|&lt;R\\},\n  \\qquad\n  Z_R=\\int_{B_R}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx,\n  \\qquad\n  d\\mu_R=Z_R^{-1}1_{B_R}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(C=4/\\varepsilon\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth even convex potential \\(\\varphi_R\\), normalized so that its unique minimum is at \\(0\\), such that \\(\\nabla\\varphi_R:\\mathbb R^n\\to B_R\\) is a diffeomorphism and\n\\[\n\\det D^2\\varphi_R\n=\nZ_R\\exp\\{V(\\nabla\\varphi_R)-\\varphi_R\\},\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\\[\n0&lt;D^2\\varphi_R\\leq CI.\n\\]\nThe measure \\(d\\nu_R=e^{-\\varphi_R}\\,dz\\) is a probability measure satisfying\n\\[\n(\\nabla\\varphi_R)_\\#\\nu_R=\\mu_R.\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(R\\), \\(\\nu_R\\) has an exponential tail, and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_R}\\,z\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi_R(z)=n.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Evenness and uniform convexity give\n\\[\nV(x)\\geq V(0)+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}|x|^2,\n\\]\nso \\(0&lt;Z&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p>The moment theorem of Berman–Berndtsson [2, Theorem 1.1], applied to \\(\\overline B_R\\) and the positive smooth density \\(e^{-V}/Z_R\\), gives a smooth convex potential \\(\\varphi_R\\), unique up to translation, for which \\(\\nabla\\varphi_R:\\mathbb R^n\\to B_R\\) is a diffeomorphism and (3.2) holds. The centering hypothesis holds by evenness. Integrating the equation through this diffeomorphism shows that \\(e^{-\\varphi_R}\\,dz\\) is a probability measure and that its image under \\(\\nabla\\varphi_R\\) is \\(\\mu_R\\).</p><p>Klartag’s estimate [19, Proposition 3.1 and Remark 3.5] gives\n\\[\n0&lt;D^2\\varphi_R\\leq CI,\n\\qquad C=4/\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nIndeed, the ball has smooth positively curved boundary, and\n\\[\n\\rho_R=V+\\log Z_R\n\\]\nis convex and smooth, with itself and all its derivatives bounded on \\(B_R\\), and \\(D^2\\rho_R\\geq\\varepsilon I\\). The constant \\(C\\) is independent of \\(R\\).</p><p>Translate the unique preimage of \\(0\\) to \\(0\\). It is the unique minimum of \\(\\varphi_R\\). Reflection gives a second potential for the same target with its minimum at \\(0\\); uniqueness up to translation therefore makes \\(\\varphi_R\\) even.</p><p>For each fixed \\(R\\), take the preimages of the slopes \\(\\pm(R/2)e_i\\). Their supporting planes give\n\\[\n\\varphi_R(z)\\geq\\frac{R}{2\\sqrt n}|z|-b_R\n\\]\nwith a finite, possibly \\(R\\)-dependent constant \\(b_R\\). Thus \\(\\nu_R\\) has an exponential tail. Since \\(|\\nabla\\varphi_R|\\leq R\\), integration of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}(ze^{-\\varphi_R})\n\\]\nwith expanding compact cutoffs is justified and yields (3.3).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniform bounds for the compact moment potentials.", "statement": "<p>Uniform bounds for the compact moment potentials.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the setting of Proposition 1 and the potentials \\(\\varphi_R\\), measures \\(\\nu_R\\), and constant \\(C=4/\\varepsilon\\) of Lemma 1.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  m_R=\\varphi_R(0),\n  \\qquad\n  g_R=\\varphi_R-m_R\\geq0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  d_0=Z^{-1}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\max_{\\overline B_1}V\\right)&gt;0,\n  \\qquad\n  c=d_0\\int_{B_1}|x_1|\\,dx&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(R\\geq2\\),\n\\[\nc|y|-n\\leq g_R(y)\\leq\\frac C2|y|^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{C}\\right)^{n/2}\n\\leq e^{m_R}\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-g_R(y)}\\,dy\n\\leq\ne^n\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-c|y|}\\,dy.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nConsequently, \\(m_R\\) is uniformly bounded on both sides, and all densities \\(e^{-\\varphi_R}\\) have one integrable exponential majorant.</p><p>On every fixed ball in the \\(z\\)-coordinates, \\(\\varphi_R\\) and \\(\\nabla\\varphi_R\\) are uniformly bounded, and there is \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), independent of \\(R\\), such that\n\\[\n\\delta I\\leq D^2\\varphi_R\\leq CI.\n\\]\nOn that ball, the functions\n\\[\nf_R=V(\\nabla\\varphi_R)+\\log Z_R-\\varphi_R\n\\]\nare uniformly Lipschitz.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Hessian bound and the normalization at the minimum imply\n\\[\ng_R(y)\\leq\\frac C2|y|^2,\n\\qquad\n|\\nabla\\varphi_R(y)|\\leq C|y|.\n\\]\nFor arbitrary \\(y,z\\), set \\(x=\\nabla\\varphi_R(z)\\). Supporting planes at \\(z\\) and \\(-z\\) give\n\\[\ng_R(y)\\geq g_R(z)+|x\\cdot y|-x\\cdot z.\n\\]\nIntegrate with respect to \\(\\nu_R\\), discard the nonnegative \\(\\mathbb E_{\\nu_R}g_R\\), and use (3.3):\n\\[\ng_R(y)\\geq\\int|x\\cdot y|\\,d\\mu_R(x)-n.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(B_1\\), the density of \\(\\mu_R\\) is at least \\(d_0\\). Rotational symmetry of the ball therefore bounds the integral below by \\(c|y|\\). This proves the two bounds for \\(g_R\\).</p><p>Since \\(\\nu_R\\) is a probability,\n\\[\ne^{m_R}=\\int e^{-g_R(y)}\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe upper and lower bounds for \\(g_R\\) give (3.4). This bounds \\(m_R\\) on both sides and gives the asserted common exponential majorant.</p><p>On each fixed ball, the preceding estimates bound \\(\\varphi_R\\) and \\(\\nabla\\varphi_R\\) uniformly. Since\n\\[\nZ_2\\leq Z_R\\leq Z,\n\\]\nequation (3.2) bounds \\(\\det D^2\\varphi_R\\) below by a positive constant there. Together with \\(D^2\\varphi_R\\leq CI\\), this gives a uniform local lower bound: if the determinant is at least \\(d&gt;0\\), every eigenvalue is at least \\(d/C^{n-1}\\).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\nabla f_R\n=\nD^2\\varphi_R\\,\\nabla V(\\nabla\\varphi_R)-\\nabla\\varphi_R\n\\]\nis uniformly bounded on that ball, so \\(f_R\\) is uniformly Lipschitz.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Adjoints and compact Hessian identity in moment coordinates — Section 4, equations (4.1)–(4.7).", "statement": "<p>Adjoints and compact Hessian identity in moment coordinates — Section 4, equations (4.1)–(4.7).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq2\\), let \\(V\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n)\\) be even with \\(D^2V\\geq\\varepsilon I\\), \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and let\n  \\[\n  d\\mu(x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varphi\\) be the potential supplied by Proposition 1, and write\n  \\[\n  d\\nu(z)=e^{-\\varphi(z)}\\,dz,\n  \\qquad\n  x=\\nabla\\varphi(z),\n  \\qquad\n  \\tau=D_z^2\\varphi,\n  \\qquad\n  M=\\tau^{-1}.\n  \\]\n  In target coordinates, matrix fields are evaluated at \\(z=(\\nabla\\varphi)^{-1}(x)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Expectations under the two measures are identified by the moment map and denoted by \\(\\mathbb E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Repeated indices are summed from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\). Put\n  \\[\n  \\partial_k=\\partial_{z_k},\n  \\qquad\n  \\partial_k^*=x_k-\\partial_k.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define negative weighted divergence by\n  \\[\n  \\delta_\\mu W\n  =\n  \\sum_j\\big(V_{x_j}W_j-\\partial_{x_j}W_j\\big).\n  \\]\n  On matrices, \\(\\delta_\\mu\\) acts on each row.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R_x^n)\\), define\n  \\[\n  Af=\\delta_\\mu(\\tau\\nabla_xf),\n  \\qquad\n  h=D_x^2f,\n  \\qquad\n  P=\\tau h,\n  \\qquad\n  B=\\tau h\\tau,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  W_a=\\partial_k^*P_{ak},\n  \\qquad\n  u=\\delta_\\mu W.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every smooth row field \\(P\\),\n\\[\n\\partial_k^*P_{ak}=(\\delta_\\mu(P\\tau))_a.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\delta_\\mu\\tau=x,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nAf\n=\n\\partial_k^*f_{x_k}\n=\nx\\cdot\\nabla_xf-\\tau:D_x^2f.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nFor compact smooth tests \\(f,g\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[gAf]\n=\n\\mathbb E[\\nabla_xg\\cdot\\tau\\nabla_xf].\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nThe fields constructed from \\(f\\) have compact support in both coordinate systems and satisfy\n\\[\nW=\\delta_\\mu B,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[uF]\n=\n\\mathbb E[W\\cdot\\nabla_xF]\n=\n\\mathbb E[B:D_x^2F]\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nfor every smooth target function \\(F\\), whether or not \\(F\\) is compactly supported. Moreover,\n\\[\nW=\\nabla_z\\big[((A-1)f)(x(z))\\big]\n=\\tau\\nabla_x(A-1)f,\n\\qquad\nu=A(A-1)f,\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(Af)(A-1)f]\n=\n\\mathbb E\\left\\|\\tau^{1/2}D_x^2f\\,\\tau^{1/2}\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\operatorname{tr}(Q^TQ)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The operator \\(\\partial_k^*=x_k-\\partial_k\\) is the adjoint of \\(\\partial_k\\) in \\(L^2(\\nu)\\), because \\(\\partial_k\\varphi=x_k\\). For a smooth row field \\(P_{ak}\\) and a compact test \\(g\\), the chain rule and adjunction give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[g\\,\\partial_k^*P_{ak}]\n=\n\\mathbb E[(\\partial_kg)P_{ak}]\n=\n\\mathbb E[g_{x_i}\\tau_{ik}P_{ak}].\n\\]\nThis proves (4.1). Taking \\(P\\) to be the identity matrix gives \\(\\delta_\\mu\\tau=x\\), or equivalently\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[x_jg]=\\mathbb E[\\tau_{jk}g_{x_k}].\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nAf\n=\n\\delta_\\mu(\\tau\\nabla_xf)\n=\n\\partial_k^*f_{x_k}\n=\nx\\cdot\\nabla_xf-\\tau:D_x^2f,\n\\]\nand adjunction gives (4.3). These formulas involve no operator-domain assertion. In particular,\n\\[\nA1=0,\\qquad Ax_j=x_j.\n\\]</p><p>All the fields constructed from \\(f\\) have compact support in both coordinates, by the diffeomorphism and its continuous inverse. Equation (4.1) gives \\(W=\\delta_\\mu B\\). Two adjunctions then give (4.5).</p><p>To verify (4.6), put\n\\[\nC_{ijk}=\\partial_{z_i}\\partial_{z_j}\\partial_{z_k}\\varphi.\n\\]\nThis tensor is fully symmetric. Expansion gives\n\\[\nW_a\n=\nx_k\\tau_{ai}f_{x_ix_k}\n-\nC_{aik}f_{x_ix_k}\n-\n\\tau_{ai}\\tau_{jk}f_{x_ix_kx_j}.\n\\]\nDifferentiating \\((A-1)f\\) in \\(z_a\\) gives exactly these terms: the first-gradient terms from \\(x\\cdot\\nabla_xf\\) and \\(-f\\) cancel. Thus\n\\[\nW=\\tau\\nabla_x(A-1)f,\n\\]\nand the formula for \\(u\\) follows from (4.2). In particular, \\(D_zW\\) is symmetric.</p><p>Finally, two compact integrations by parts give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E[(Af)(A-1)f]\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E[\\nabla_xf\\cdot\\tau\\nabla_x(A-1)f]\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E[\\nabla_xf\\cdot W]\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E[h:B]\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E\\left\\|\\tau^{1/2}D_x^2f\\,\\tau^{1/2}\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis is (4.7). Since \\(A1=0\\) and \\(Ax_j=x_j\\), the expression \\(A(A-1)\\) annihilates every affine function.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Density of even tests — original Lemma 5.1.", "statement": "<p>Density of even tests — original Lemma 5.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\mu=\\rho(x)\\,dx\\) be a probability measure on \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) with a smooth, strictly positive, even density.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\tau\\) be a smooth even symmetric matrix field satisfying\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\tau(x)\\leq\\Lambda I\n  \\]\n  for some finite \\(\\Lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  Af=x\\cdot\\nabla f-\\tau:D^2f.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the integration formula\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb E[hAf]\n  =\n  \\mathbb E[\\nabla h\\cdot\\tau\\nabla f],\n  \\qquad\n  f,h\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n),\n  \\]\n  and the compact Hessian identity\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb E[(Af)(A-1)f]\n  =\n  \\mathbb E\\left\\|\\tau^{1/2}D^2f\\,\\tau^{1/2}\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2,\n  \\qquad\n  f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\overline{\n\\{A(A-1)f:f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n),\\ f\\text{ even}\\}\n}^{\\,L^2(\\mu)}\n=\n\\{g\\in L^2(\\mu):g\\text{ even},\\ \\mathbb Eg=0\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The integration formula also holds when one factor is smooth and the other has compact support, by inserting a cutoff around that support. In particular,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[Ak]=0\n\\]\nfor compactly supported smooth \\(k\\). Since \\(A\\) preserves parity, the displayed range in Proposition 3 consists of centered even functions.</p><p>Let \\(g\\) be a centered even \\(L^2(\\mu)\\) function orthogonal to this range. It also annihilates \\(A(A-1)f\\) for odd \\(f\\), because that image is odd. Formal symmetry therefore gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal Pg=0\n\\quad\\text{in distributions},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal P=A(A-1).\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(\\mathcal P^t\\) denotes the transpose with respect to Lebesgue measure and \\(d\\mu=\\rho\\,dx\\), weighted formal symmetry gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal P^t(\\rho g)=\\rho\\mathcal Pg\n\\]\nin distributions. Orthogonality makes the left side zero, and smooth positivity of \\(\\rho\\) gives the displayed equation.</p><p>Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2 show that \\(g\\) is affine. Since \\(g\\) is even, it is constant, and its mean is zero. Hence \\(g=0\\). Taking the orthogonal complement in the centered even subspace proves the density assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Regularity and \\(L^2\\) splitting of the distributional equation.", "statement": "<p>Regularity and \\(L^2\\) splitting of the distributional equation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit \\(\\mu,\\tau,A\\) and the two identities assumed in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma(f,h)=\\nabla f\\cdot\\tau\\nabla h,\n  \\qquad\n  \\Gamma(f)=\\Gamma(f,f),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal P=A(A-1).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\in L^2(\\mu)\\) satisfy \\(\\mathcal Pg=0\\) in distributions. No evenness or centering is required in this lemma.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(g\\) is smooth, \\(v=Ag\\) belongs to \\(L^2(\\mu)\\), and\n\\[\nw=g-v\\in L^2(\\mu),\n\\qquad\nAw=0,\n\\qquad\nAv=v.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The principal symbol of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\n\\[\n(\\xi\\cdot\\tau(x)\\xi)^2,\n\\]\nwhich is positive for every \\(\\xi\\neq0\\). Interior elliptic regularity gives \\(g\\in C^\\infty\\) [14, Theorem 14.2]. Only positivity on compact sets is needed here.</p><p>Choose \\(0\\leq\\eta_r\\leq1\\) smooth, equal to one on \\(B_r\\), zero outside \\(B_{2r}\\), with\n\\[\n|\\nabla\\eta_r|\\leq C/r,\n\\qquad\n|D^2\\eta_r|\\leq C/r^2,\n\\qquad r\\geq1.\n\\]\nThe upper bound on \\(\\tau\\) gives constants \\(B,K\\), independent of \\(r\\), such that\n\\[\n\\Gamma(\\eta_r)^{1/2}\\leq B/r,\n\\qquad\n|A\\eta_r|\\leq K.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nFor the second estimate, use \\(|x|\\leq2r\\) on the support of the cutoff derivatives. Those derivatives, including \\(A\\eta_r\\), vanish outside \\(B_{2r}\\setminus B_r\\).</p><p>Put \\(v=Ag\\), initially just a smooth function, and write\n\\[\nG=\\|g\\|_2,\n\\qquad\nX_r=\\|\\eta_r^2v\\|_2,\n\\qquad\nY_r^2=\\mathbb E[\\eta_r^2\\Gamma(g)].\n\\]\nHere \\(G\\) is finite by hypothesis, while \\(X_r,Y_r\\) are finite by compact support.</p><p>Testing \\(\\mathcal Pg=0\\) against \\(\\eta_r^4g\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[v(A-1)(\\eta_r^4g)]=0.\n\\]\nThe product rule\n\\[\nA(ab)=aAb+bAa-2\\Gamma(a,b)\n\\]\nyields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(A-1)(\\eta_r^4g)\n={}&amp;\n\\eta_r^4v\n+\ng\\big(4\\eta_r^3A\\eta_r\n-12\\eta_r^2\\Gamma(\\eta_r)-\\eta_r^4\\big)\\\\\n&amp;-8\\eta_r^3\\Gamma(\\eta_r,g).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus Cauchy–Schwarz and (5.1) imply\n\\[\nX_r\\leq aG+\\frac{8B}{r}Y_r,\n\\qquad\nY_r^2\\leq GX_r+\\frac{2B}{r}GY_r,\n\\qquad\na=1+4K+12B^2.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nThe first inequality is immediate if \\(X_r=0\\); otherwise divide the resulting estimate for \\(X_r^2\\) by \\(X_r\\). For the second, integrate by parts in \\(\\mathbb E[\\eta_r^2gAg]\\):\n\\[\nY_r^2\n=\n\\mathbb E[\\eta_r^2gv]\n-\n2\\mathbb E[\\eta_rg\\Gamma(\\eta_r,g)].\n\\]</p><p>Combining (5.2) gives\n\\[\nY_r^2\\leq aG^2+10BGY_r,\n\\]\nso both \\(Y_r\\) and \\(X_r\\) are bounded uniformly in \\(r\\). Exhaustion by balls proves \\(v=Ag\\in L^2(\\mu)\\).</p><p>Now set \\(w=g-v\\). The equation \\(\\mathcal Pg=0\\) gives \\(Av=v\\), hence \\(Aw=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Classification of the harmonic and eigenvalue-one parts.", "statement": "<p>Classification of the harmonic and eigenvalue-one parts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit \\(\\mu,\\tau,A,\\Gamma\\) from Proposition 3 and Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w,v\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n)\\cap L^2(\\mu)\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  Aw=0,\\qquad Av=v.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(w\\) is constant, and \\(v\\) is linear with zero constant term. Consequently, every \\(L^2(\\mu)\\) distributional solution of \\(A(A-1)g=0\\) is affine.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the cutoffs \\(\\eta_r\\) and estimates (5.1) from Lemma 3.1.</p><p>Testing \\(Aw=0\\) with \\(\\eta_r^2w\\) gives\n\\[\n\\big(\\mathbb E[\\eta_r^2\\Gamma(w)]\\big)^{1/2}\n\\leq\\frac{2B}{r}\\|w\\|_2.\n\\]\nOn any fixed ball, let \\(r\\to\\infty\\). Then \\(\\Gamma(w)=0\\) there; strict positivity of \\(\\tau\\) shows that \\(w\\) is constant.</p><p>For \\(v\\), the analogous test of \\(Av=v\\), with\n\\[\nZ_r^2=\\mathbb E[\\eta_r^2\\Gamma(v)],\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nZ_r^2\n\\leq\n\\|v\\|_2^2+\\frac{2B}{r}\\|v\\|_2Z_r.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\mathbb E\\Gamma(v)&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p>For \\(f_r=\\eta_rv\\in C_c^\\infty\\), the product rule proves\n\\[\nf_r\\longrightarrow v,\n\\qquad\nAf_r\\longrightarrow v\n\\quad\\text{in }L^2(\\mu).\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\nAf_r=\\eta_rv+vA\\eta_r-2\\Gamma(\\eta_r,v).\n\\]\nThe middle term has norm at most\n\\[\nK\\|v1_{B_{2r}\\setminus B_r}\\|_2\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nFor the last term,\n\\[\n\\|\\Gamma(\\eta_r,v)\\|_2\n\\leq\n\\frac Br\\big(\\mathbb E\\Gamma(v)\\big)^{1/2}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p><p>Apply the compact Hessian identity to \\(f_r\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\n\\left\\|\\tau^{1/2}D^2f_r\\,\\tau^{1/2}\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n=\n\\|Af_r\\|_2^2-\\mathbb E[f_rAf_r]\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nOn each fixed ball, \\(f_r=v\\) for all sufficiently large \\(r\\). Positivity of the density and of \\(\\tau\\) therefore forces \\(D^2v=0\\) on that ball. Thus \\(v\\) is affine, and \\(Av=v\\) removes its constant term.</p><p>The final assertion follows from the splitting in Lemma 3.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Moment-coordinate tensor estimate — original Lemma 6.1.", "statement": "<p>Moment-coordinate tensor estimate — original Lemma 6.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  d\\mu(x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx,\n  \\qquad\n  d\\nu(z)=e^{-\\varphi(z)}\\,dz\n  \\]\n  be smooth probability measures on \\(\\mathbb R^n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(D_z^2\\varphi\\) is positive definite and\n  \\[\n  x=\\nabla\\varphi(z)\n  \\]\n  is a smooth diffeomorphism pushing \\(\\nu\\) to \\(\\mu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">These assumptions do not require evenness or a global upper Hessian bound.</p><p class=\"li\">For a real \\(f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R_x^n)\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\tau=D_z^2\\varphi,\\qquad\n  M=\\tau^{-1},\\qquad\n  H=D_x^2V,\\qquad\n  h=D_x^2f,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  B=\\tau h\\tau,\\qquad\n  W=\\delta_\\mu B,\\qquad\n  u=\\delta_\\mu W.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Here\n  \\[\n  \\delta_\\mu W=\\sum_j(V_{x_j}W_j-\\partial_{x_j}W_j),\n  \\]\n  and \\(\\delta_\\mu\\) acts on each row of a matrix.</p><p class=\"li\">View source fields in target coordinates by the inverse moment map. Expectations are identified under the moment map and denoted by \\(\\mathbb E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\((D_xW)_{ij}=\\partial_{x_j}W_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}\\big((D_xW)^2\\big)\n\\geq\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBHB),\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb Eu^2\n\\geq\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBHB)\n+\n\\mathbb E[W^THW].\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nThe square in (6.1) is a matrix square.</p>", "proof": "<p>Subtract (6.6) from (6.5). The common first term cancels, and Lemma 4.3 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}\\big((D_xW)^2\\big)\n-\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBHB)\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\n=\n\\mathbb E\\mathcal D+\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MRMR)\n\\geq0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis proves (6.1).</p><p>For any compactly supported smooth vector field \\(W\\), with \\(u=\\delta_\\mu W\\),\n\\[\n\\partial_{x_i}u\n=\nH_{ij}W_j+(V_{x_j}-\\partial_{x_j})\\partial_{x_i}W_j.\n\\]\nPairing with \\(W_i\\) and using adjunction gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb Eu^2\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E[W_i\\partial_{x_i}u]\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E[W^THW]\n+\n\\mathbb E[(\\partial_{x_j}W_i)(\\partial_{x_i}W_j)]\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E[W^THW]\n+\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}\\big((D_xW)^2\\big).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(6.3)\n\\]\nCombining (6.3) with (6.1) proves (6.2).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Differentiated moment equation.", "statement": "<p>Differentiated moment equation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the moment measures, moment map, and matrix fields \\(\\tau,M,H\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\partial_i=\\partial_{z_i},\n  \\qquad\n  C_{ijk}=\\partial_{z_i}\\partial_{z_j}\\partial_{z_k}\\varphi,\n  \\qquad\n  (C_i)_{kl}=C_{ikl}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n(\\tau H\\tau-\\tau)_{ij}\n=\n(\\partial_d-x_d)(M_{dk}C_{ijk})\n-\n\\operatorname{tr}(MC_iMC_j).\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The moment equation is\n\\[\nV(x(z))\n=\n\\varphi(z)+\\log\\det\\tau(z)-\\log Z.\n\\]\nIts first derivative gives\n\\[\n\\tau_{ik}V_{x_k}=x_i+c_i,\n\\qquad\nc_i=\\operatorname{tr}(MC_i).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\partial_jM=-MC_jM,\n\\]\ndifferentiating once more gives\n\\[\n(\\tau H\\tau)_{ij}\n=\n\\tau_{ij}\n+\nM_{kl}\\varphi_{klij}\n-\n\\operatorname{tr}(MC_iMC_j)\n-\nC_{ijk}V_{x_k}.\n\\]</p><p>To combine the fourth-derivative term and the term involving \\(\\nabla_xV\\) into a weighted divergence, note that\n\\[\n\\partial_dM_{dk}\n=\n-M_{da}C_{abd}M_{bk}\n=\n-c_bM_{bk},\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\partial_dM_{dk}-x_dM_{dk}=-V_{x_k}.\n\\]\nFull symmetry of \\(D_z^4\\varphi\\) therefore gives (6.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Two integrated tensor expansions.", "statement": "<p>Two integrated tensor expansions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the setting of Proposition 4 and the notation \\(C_i\\) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  P=\\tau h,\n  \\qquad\n  \\partial_k^*=x_k-\\partial_{z_k},\n  \\qquad\n  R_{al}=\\partial_k^*\\partial_lP_{ak},\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  Q=MBMBM=h\\tau h.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrix \\(R\\) is symmetric,\n\\[\nD_zW=B+R,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}\\big((D_xW)^2\\big)\n={}&amp;\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBMB)\\\\\n&amp;+\n2\\mathbb E[(\\partial_kh_{al})(\\partial_lP_{ak})]\n+\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MRMR).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(6.5)\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBHB)\n={}&amp;\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBMB)\\\\\n&amp;-\n\\mathbb E[(\\partial_dQ_{ij})M_{dk}C_{ijk}]\n-\n\\mathbb E[Q_{ij}\\operatorname{tr}(MC_iMC_j)].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(6.6)\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(MRMR)\n=\n\\|M^{1/2}RM^{1/2}\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\\geq0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>All test fields used below have compact support in both coordinate systems. The chain rule and adjunction give\n\\[\nW_a=\\partial_k^*P_{ak}.\n\\]\nThe direct expansion is\n\\[\nW_a\n=\nx_k\\tau_{ai}f_{x_ix_k}\n-\nC_{aik}f_{x_ix_k}\n-\n\\tau_{ai}\\tau_{jk}f_{x_ix_kx_j}.\n\\]\nAs in (4.6), this is the \\(z_a\\)-derivative of\n\\[\n\\big(x\\cdot\\nabla_xf-\\tau:D_x^2f-f\\big)(x(z)).\n\\]\nThus \\(D_zW\\) is symmetric.</p><p>The commutator\n\\[\n[\\partial_l,\\partial_k^*]=\\tau_{kl}\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nD_zW=B+R.\n\\]\nBoth \\(D_zW\\) and \\(B\\) are symmetric, so \\(R\\) is symmetric. Since\n\\[\nD_xW=(B+R)M,\n\\qquad\nMBM=h,\n\\]\nexpansion followed by one integration by parts gives (6.5). The last term is nonnegative because\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(MRMR)\n=\n\\|M^{1/2}RM^{1/2}\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\]</p><p>In the contraction \\(\\operatorname{tr}(MBHB)\\), the coefficient of \\(\\tau H\\tau\\) is\n\\[\nQ=MBMBM=h\\tau h.\n\\]\nSubstitute (6.4) from Lemma 4.1 and integrate its divergence term by parts. This gives (6.6).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Nonnegative tensor remainder.", "statement": "<p>Nonnegative tensor remainder.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the fields \\(h,P,Q,M,C\\) from Proposition 4 and Lemmas 4.1–4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the contracted scalar\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\mathcal D={}&amp;\n  2(\\partial_kh_{al})(\\partial_lP_{ak})\n  +(\\partial_dQ_{ij})M_{dk}C_{ijk}\\\\\n  &amp;+Q_{ij}\\operatorname{tr}(MC_iMC_j).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every point, \\(\\mathcal D\\geq0\\). More precisely, in constant dual coordinates normalized so that \\(\\tau=I\\) at that point, put\n\\[\nS_{ijk}=f_{x_ix_jx_k},\n\\qquad\nJ_{ijk}=h_{ia}C_{ajk},\n\\qquad\nJ^{(12)}_{ijk}=J_{jik},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{Sym}J)_{ijk}\n=\n\\frac{J_{ijk}+J_{jik}+J_{kij}}3.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\n=\n2\\|S+\\operatorname{Sym}J\\|^2\n+\n\\frac16\\|J-J^{(12)}\\|^2\n\\geq0.\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nThe tensor norms in this formula are Euclidean norms in the normalized coordinates.</p>", "proof": "<p>The normalization uses a constant dual change of coordinates. For an invertible constant matrix \\(T\\), set\n\\[\nz=Tz',\n\\qquad\nx'=T^Tx,\n\\qquad\nf'(x')=f(T^{-T}x').\n\\]\nThe corresponding potentials and partition function are\n\\[\n\\varphi'(z')=\\varphi(Tz')-\\log|\\det T|,\n\\]\n\\[\nV'(x')=V(T^{-T}x'),\n\\qquad\nZ'=|\\det T|Z.\n\\]\nThey preserve both probability measures by change of variables, the relation \\(x'=\\nabla_{z'}\\varphi'\\), and the moment equation.</p><p>The chain rule gives\n\\[\n\\tau'=T^T\\tau T,\n\\qquad\nB'=T^TBT,\n\\qquad\nR'=T^TRT,\n\\]\n\\[\nM'=T^{-1}MT^{-T},\n\\qquad\nh'=T^{-1}hT^{-T},\n\\qquad\nH'=T^{-1}HT^{-T},\n\\]\n\\[\nQ'=T^{-1}QT^{-T},\n\\qquad\nP'=T^TPT^{-T},\n\\qquad\nW'=T^TW.\n\\]\nEach \\(z\\)-derivative index, including all three indices of \\(C\\), transforms covariantly with \\(T\\); the same law holds for \\(\\partial_k^*\\). Also,\n\\[\nD_{x'}W'=T^T(D_xW)T^{-T}.\n\\]\nEvery contracted scalar in (6.5) and (6.6) is therefore invariant. For example, in\n\\[\n(\\partial_kh_{al})(\\partial_lP_{ak}),\n\\]\neach index pairs one covariant and one contravariant factor.</p><p>At a fixed point take \\(T=\\tau^{-1/2}\\), using the value of \\(\\tau\\) at that point. All derivatives are computed under this constant transformation. No moving frame is differentiated; the integrations by parts have already been completed.</p><p>In the normalized coordinates, \\(S\\) is fully symmetric and \\(J\\) is symmetric in its last two indices. The three contractions are\n\\[\n2(\\partial_kh_{al})(\\partial_lP_{ak})\n=\n2\\|S\\|^2+2\\langle S,J\\rangle,\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\partial_dQ_{ij})C_{ijd}\n=\n2\\langle S,J\\rangle+\\langle J,J^{(12)}\\rangle,\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nQ_{ij}\\operatorname{tr}(C_iC_j)=\\|J\\|^2.\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]</p><p>For (6.7), use\n\\[\n\\partial_kh_{al}=S_{alk},\n\\qquad\n\\partial_lP_{ak}=C_{ail}h_{ik}+S_{akl}.\n\\]\nFor (6.8), differentiating the two factors \\(h\\) in \\(Q=h\\tau h\\) gives the two copies of \\(\\langle S,J\\rangle\\). Differentiating its middle factor gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i,a,b,j,d}h_{ia}C_{abd}h_{bj}C_{ijd}\n=\n\\sum_d\\operatorname{tr}(hC_dhC_d)\n=\n\\langle J,J^{(12)}\\rangle.\n\\]\nEquation (6.9) follows by expanding \\(Q=h^2\\) and the trace, using the full symmetry of \\(C\\).</p><p>Thus\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\n=\n2\\|S\\|^2+4\\langle S,J\\rangle\n+\\|J\\|^2+\\langle J,J^{(12)}\\rangle.\n\\]\nFull symmetrization satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\operatorname{Sym}J\\|^2\n=\n\\frac{\\|J\\|^2+2\\langle J,J^{(12)}\\rangle}{3},\n\\qquad\n\\langle S,\\operatorname{Sym}J\\rangle=\\langle S,J\\rangle.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\n=\n2\\|S+\\operatorname{Sym}J\\|^2\n+\n\\frac16\\|J-J^{(12)}\\|^2\n\\geq0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Variance bound — original Proposition 2.1.", "statement": "<p>Variance bound — original Proposition 2.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq2\\), \\(N\\geq1\\), let \\(q\\geq2\\) be an even integer, and let \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  p_1,\\ldots,p_N\\in\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\{0\\},\n  \\qquad\n  h_1,\\ldots,h_N&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\psi_i(x)=\\left(\\frac{\\langle p_i,x\\rangle}{h_i}\\right)^q,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  V(x)=\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}|x|^2+\\sum_{i=1}^N\\psi_i(x),\n  \\qquad\n  Z=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx,\n  \\qquad\n  d\\mu=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\\,dx.\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb E_\\mu F=\\int F\\,d\\mu,\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{Var}_\\mu(F)=\\mathbb E_\\mu(F-\\mathbb E_\\mu F)^2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all real \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_N\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}_\\mu\\!\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^Nb_i\\psi_i\\right)\n\\leq\n\\sum_{i=1}^Nb_i^2\\,\\mathbb E_\\mu\\psi_i.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nThe Gaussian term makes \\(Z\\) finite even if the \\(p_i\\) do not span \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), and all polynomial moments are finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>The potential \\(V\\) is smooth and even, and \\(D^2V\\geq\\varepsilon I\\). Proposition 1 therefore supplies moment coordinates with\n\\[\n0&lt;\\tau\\leq(4/\\varepsilon)I.\n\\]\nAll expectations below are with respect to \\(\\mu\\).</p><p>Take an even \\(f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n)\\), and form \\(B,W,u\\) as in Lemma 5.2. Since\n\\[\nH=D^2V=\\varepsilon I+\\sum_iH_i,\n\\]\nProposition 4 and Lemma 5.2 imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb Eu^2\n&amp;\\geq\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBHB)+\\mathbb E[W^THW]\\\\\n&amp;\\geq\n\\sum_i\n\\left(\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(MBH_iB)\n+\n\\mathbb E[W^TH_iW]\n\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\geq\n\\sum_i\\frac{qd_i^2}{m_i}\n=\n\\sum_i\\frac{d_i^2}{a_i}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(7.6)\n\\]\nThe discarded terms are nonnegative, since \\(M\\) is positive definite and \\(B\\) is symmetric.</p><p>By Proposition 2, the operator \\(A\\) satisfies the integration formula and compact Hessian identity required in Proposition 3. The measure \\(\\mu\\) has a smooth strictly positive even density, and \\(\\tau\\) is smooth, even, strictly positive, and bounded above. Proposition 3 therefore shows that these functions \\(u=A(A-1)f\\) are dense in the centered even subspace of \\(L^2(\\mu)\\).</p><p>Each \\(\\psi_i\\in L^2(\\mu)\\), so the pairings\n\\[\nu\\longmapsto\\mathbb E[u\\psi_i]\n\\]\nare continuous. Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_i\n\\frac{(\\mathbb E[u\\psi_i])^2}{\\mathbb E\\psi_i}\n\\leq\n\\mathbb Eu^2\n\\]\nfor every centered even \\(u\\in L^2(\\mu)\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nF=\\sum_i b_i\\psi_i,\n\\qquad\nu=F-\\mathbb EF,\n\\qquad\nd_i=\\mathbb E[u\\psi_i],\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|u\\|_2^2\n&amp;=\n\\sum_i b_id_i\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\left(\\sum_i b_i^2a_i\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\sum_i d_i^2/a_i\\right)^{1/2}\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\left(\\sum_i b_i^2a_i\\right)^{1/2}\\|u\\|_2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\|u\\|_2=0\\), the conclusion is immediate; otherwise division and squaring prove (2.2).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Homogeneity and moment integration for the summands.", "statement": "<p>Homogeneity and moment integration for the summands.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit \\(V,\\mu,\\psi_i,q,\\varepsilon\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varphi\\) be the moment potential supplied by Proposition 1 for \\(V\\), and in target coordinates put\n  \\[\n  \\tau(x)=D_z^2\\varphi((\\nabla\\varphi)^{-1}(x)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(1\\leq i\\leq N\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_i=D_x^2\\psi_i,\n  \\qquad\n  a_i=\\mathbb E_\\mu\\psi_i,\n  \\qquad\n  m_i=\\mathbb E_\\mu\\operatorname{tr}(\\tau H_i).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(H_i\\) is positive semidefinite and \\(a_i&gt;0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nx\\cdot\\nabla\\psi_i=q\\psi_i,\n\\qquad\nH_ix=(q-1)\\nabla\\psi_i,\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nm_i\n=\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[x\\cdot\\nabla\\psi_i]\n=\nqa_i.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Each \\(H_i\\) is positive semidefinite, since \\(q\\) is even. Also \\(a_i&gt;0\\), since \\(p_i\\neq0\\) and \\(\\mu\\) has positive density everywhere. Homogeneity gives (7.1).</p><p>Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[x_jk]\n=\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[\\tau_{jk}\\partial_{x_k}k]\n\\]\nfor compact smooth \\(k\\). To justify its use for the derivatives of \\(\\psi_i\\), apply it to\n\\[\nk=\\eta_R\\partial_{x_j}\\psi_i\n\\]\nand sum in \\(j\\), where \\(\\eta_R\\) is a smooth cutoff equal to one on \\(B_R\\) and zero outside \\(B_{2R}\\).</p><p>The extra term is bounded in absolute value by\n\\[\nCR^{-1}\n\\mathbb E_\\mu\\!\\left[\n|\\nabla\\psi_i|\\,1_{\\{|x|\\geq R\\}}\n\\right],\n\\]\nwhich tends to zero. The other terms converge because \\(\\tau\\) is bounded and all polynomial moments are finite. Thus\n\\[\nm_i\n=\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[x\\cdot\\nabla\\psi_i]\n=\nqa_i.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Two complementary Cauchy–Schwarz estimates.", "statement": "<p>Two complementary Cauchy–Schwarz estimates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the potential and measure of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose moment coordinates from Proposition 1, with\n  \\[\n  \\tau=D_z^2\\varphi,\\qquad M=\\tau^{-1},\n  \\]\n  evaluated in target coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(i\\), put\n  \\[\n  H_i=D_x^2\\psi_i,\\qquad\n  a_i=\\mathbb E_\\mu\\psi_i,\\qquad\n  m_i=\\mathbb E_\\mu\\operatorname{tr}(\\tau H_i)=qa_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an even \\(f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n)\\), define\n  \\[\n  B=\\tau D_x^2f\\,\\tau,\n  \\qquad\n  W=\\delta_\\mu B,\n  \\qquad\n  u=\\delta_\\mu W=A(A-1)f,\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  A=x\\cdot\\nabla_x-\\tau:D_x^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(d_i=\\mathbb E_\\mu[u\\psi_i]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(i\\),\n\\[\nd_i\n=\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[W\\cdot\\nabla\\psi_i]\n=\n\\mathbb E_\\mu\\operatorname{tr}(BH_i),\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\\[\nd_i^2\n\\leq\nm_i\\,\\mathbb E_\\mu\\operatorname{tr}(MBH_iB),\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[W^TH_iW]\n\\geq\n(q-1)\\frac{d_i^2}{m_i}.\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the double-adjunction identity (4.5) from Proposition 2 to \\(F=\\psi_i\\). The test fields are compactly supported, so \\(F\\) need not be. This gives (7.3).</p><p>Apply Hilbert–Schmidt Cauchy–Schwarz, including integration, to the matrix fields\n\\[\nH_i^{1/2}\\tau^{1/2},\n\\qquad\nH_i^{1/2}B\\tau^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nTheir pairing is \\(\\operatorname{tr}(H_iB)\\), so\n\\[\nd_i^2\n\\leq\nm_i\\,\\mathbb E_\\mu\\operatorname{tr}(MBH_iB).\n\\]\nThis does not require \\(B\\) to be positive semidefinite.</p><p>Next, (7.1)–(7.3) give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[x^TH_ix]=(q-1)m_i,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[x^TH_iW]=(q-1)d_i.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz for the positive semidefinite form\n\\[\n(X,Y)\\longmapsto\\mathbb E_\\mu[X^TH_iY]\n\\]\ntherefore yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[W^TH_iW]\n\\geq\n(q-1)\\frac{d_i^2}{m_i}.\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 15, "cite"], [3, 16, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 14, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [6, 16, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [8, 3, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [15, 7, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"]]});
